This empty `catch {}` block silently swallows all errors thrown by `getGitDir()`, `watchFile()`, or `configureBranchWatcher()`. The project rule is clear: even for best-effort, non-fatal code paths, the caught error must be logged at minimum at `logger.debug` level before continuing. The comment explains the *intent* but the implementation discards the error object entirely, making it impossible to diagnose why watching stopped working for a user.

Fix:
```typescript
} catch (error) {
  logger.debug(
    `Change detection git watcher setup failed: ${
      error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)
    }. Scanning will continue from module graph updates.`
  );
}
```

The `.catch()` callback doesn't capture the thrown error, so the `logger.warn` message contains no diagnostic information. Per the project rule, captured errors must be logged, but even without a named binding the actual failure reason is lost here. Change to:
```typescript
void this.configureBranchWatcher(gitDir).catch((error) => {
  logger.warn(
    `Change detection failed to reconfigure git branch watcher: ${
      error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)
    }`
  );
  this.stopWatching();
});
```

The command `diff --name-only --diff-filter=A` (without `--cached`) as a means of detecting `git add -N` (intent-to-add) files is non-obvious. `git add -N` registers a path in the index as an empty blob; the unstaged `--diff-filter=A` diff then shows it as an "added" working-tree file. Without a comment this reads like a mistake or duplicate of the `--cached` line above it. Add a brief inline explanation:
```typescript
// Detects intent-to-add files (git add -N): staged as empty blobs, so the
// unstaged diff reports them as Added in the working tree.
runGitCommand(['diff', '--name-only', '--diff-filter=A']),
```

